<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dalit</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Harijan, Panchama, Untouchables</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dalit, from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> word meaning broken or scattered, is the name of the people once called untouchables and outcastes, the lowest stratum of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, excluded from the fourfold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> caste hierarchy and seen as a fifth, the Panchama. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jyotirao-phule">Jyotirao Phule</a> used the word in the late 1880s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>, himself a Dalit, popularised it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s 1950 Constitution abolished untouchability and created reservations for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a> in legislatures, government jobs and education, and in 1997 K. R. Narayanan became the first Dalit president. Caste discrimination nonetheless remains widespread, above all in rural India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dalit.jpg" alt="Dalit" width="330" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lady Ottoline Morrell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_school_of_untouchables_near_Bangalore_by_Lady_Ottoline_Morrell_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> word dalit is rendered as broken or scattered; the same people were also called Harijans. They were excluded from the fourfold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> hierarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. Their status was tied to hereditary occupations that caste Hindus considered polluting or debased, such as working with leather, disposing of dead animals, manual scavenging and sanitation work. The economist and reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> said that untouchability entered Indian society around 400 CE, through the struggle for supremacy between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanism</a>. Several scholars have drawn parallels with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burakumin">Burakumin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paekchong">Baekjeong</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Gopal Baba Walangkar (c. 1840–1900) is generally considered the pioneer of the Dalit movement, and Ambedkar himself held him to be its progenitor. Harichand Thakur (c. 1812–1878) led the Matua organisation among the Namasudra community in the Bengal Presidency, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jyotirao-phule">Jyotirao Phule</a> (1827–1890) worked to improve conditions for Dalits; his Gulamgiri of 1873 is regarded as a seminal moment in Dalit literature. In the nineteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmo-samaj">Brahmo Samaj</a>, Arya Samaj and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakrishna-mission">Ramakrishna Mission</a> took part in the cause.</p><p>The Laxminarayan Temple in Wardha was the first upper-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> temple to welcome Dalits openly, in 1928, and the last King of Travancore issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-entry-proclamation">Temple Entry Proclamation</a> in 1936. After independence in 1947 Article 17 of the Constitution outlawed untouchability. The Untouchability (Offences) Act of 1955 was amended in the same year into the Protection of Civil Rights Act, and when neither proved effective the Scheduled Caste and Scheduled Tribe (Prevention of Atrocities) Act of 1989 followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most Dalits in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, with significant populations following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. In 1956 Ambedkar launched the Dalit Buddhist movement, and about half a million Dalits joined him in rejecting Hinduism. Mass conversions to Christianity involved groups such as the Chuhras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamar">Chamars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-india">North India</a>, the Vankars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the Pulayas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. Among Sikhs the two Dalit castes of Punjab are the Mazhabis and the Ramdasias.</p><p>The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted Scheduled Caste communities at 16.6 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s population. A study found more than 900 Dalit sub-castes, with divisions between them. Dalit literature has formed an identity across languages including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, Bangla, Hindi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, Punjabi, Tamil and Odia; its earliest identifiable writers include Madara Chennaiah, an 11th-century cobbler-saint, and Dohara Kakkaiah. Dalit communities also settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, where about a third of the immigrants arriving in 1883 are estimated to have been Dalits, and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dalit” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in the 19th century; in 1956 B. R. Ambedkar, the jurist and campaigner for the rights of the <b>Dalits</b>, became a Buddhist at Nagpur with hundreds of thousands of followers; and in the 20th century Buddhist teachers and Asian migrants established communities in Europe, North America and Australia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Theologies of liberation have developed in other parts of the world: black theology in the United States and South Africa, Palestinian liberation theology, <b>Dalit</b> theology in India, Minjung theology in South Korea, liberation theology in Ireland, Deaf liberation theology and a Christian peace movement associated with liberation theology.</blockquote><a href="/movement/liberation-theology">Liberation theology · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Critics of its view of caste include the economist Jean Drèze, who calls the Hindutva project “a lifeboat for the upper castes”, and the <b>Dalit</b> activist Kancha Ilaiah, for whom “Hindutva Is Nothing But Brahminism”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>The varna scheme of the ancient texts placed the Brahmins, the priests, at the head of four orders, above the Kshatriyas (rulers and warriors), the Vaishyas (traders) and the Shudras (labourers), with <b>Dalits</b> and Adivasis outside.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>Earlier, in November 1896, the composer Julio Nakpil had written &quot;Marangal na <b>Dalit</b> ng Katagalugan&quot; as a national anthem for Andrés Bonifacio of the Katipunan, but Aguinaldo's governments never adopted it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a></li><li><blockquote>Facing resistance to his campaign for the acceptance of <b>Dalits</b> in Hindu society, he cited it in his weekly Navajivan: a religion that had produced a devotee who saw all as equal could not hold some people untouchable.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vaishnava-jana-to">Vaishnava Jana To</a></li></ul></section>
